# Ctor propagation: parser-layer realisation of "fundament-witness ↔ all-rung presence"

A small parser change — propagate body ctor names to outer
`tcBinders` when a `data` declaration finishes — collapses a
chunk of design space:

## What the change fixes

Before: in `data Nat { Z : Nat; S : Nat -> Nat }; data Fin (n :
Nat) : *0 { FZ : Fin (S n) }`, the `S` in `Fin (S n)` parsed as
`Var "S"` — the unresolved fallback.  HypLinf would reject it
with `Unbound`.

After: `S` parses as `TyConRef "S" SPath` — a type-level
constructor, addressed at Nat's body slot 1.  Same identifier as
Nat's ctor S, just at one rung up the cover.

## Why it's deeper than a "fix"

This is the parser realising what the covering-space framing
already promises algebraically.  Each value-level ctor `c` is
ALSO a type at one rung up — fundament-witness propagating to
every upper rung.  The parser was *hiding* this by scoping ctors
to their parent's body and dropping them on body close.
Propagating them out exposes the algebra's invariant at the
syntactic layer.

The covering-space framing said: a single fundament-level
registration witnesses presence at every rung.  The parser,
once corrected, says the same — once a ctor exists in
`hypEnvCtors`, it's *also* a type-level constructor in
`tcBinders`, and the two namespaces share the identifier.

## Bonus: uppercase-convention is moot

Before this change, the question hung: should we adopt
Haskell/Ωmega's lexical case convention (uppercase = fixed
tycon ref, lowercase = parameter binding) so that `data Head
Param { … }` could mean "Head indexed by Param" without the
`(P : Param)` kind-annotation ceremony?

After this change: no.  The kind-annotated-param syntax `(P :
K)` already gives users the explicit "P inhabits K" expression.
Ctor propagation handles the *other* half of the use case
(ctors referenced as type-level constructors, like `S n`).
Bare params remain variables; case-based magic isn't needed.

This is the second time in this arc that a queued
"convention/promotion ceremony" turned out to be moot once the
covering-space framing was followed through:

1. **Iso-cute `One : One`** — looked like DataKinds-style
   promotion was needed; turned out the level-coordinate already
   does it.  (Recorded on 78f00f4.)
2. **Uppercase convention** — looked like a lexical convention
   was needed for fixed-type-in-param-slot; turned out
   `(P : K)` + ctor propagation cover both cases.  (Recorded
   here.)

Pattern: each time we reach for an extra mechanism, examining
whether the substrate already supplies the answer reveals that
it does.  The covering-space framing is *load-bearing* — it
keeps doing more work than we credit it for, and the syntactic
ceremonies we'd otherwise need are simply absorbed.

## What's still pending

Even with `S` resolving as `TyConRef`, end-to-end elaboration of
`Fin (S n)` trips at HypLinf's homogeneous-app check: Fin is at
level 1 (a type at *0's predecessor); S is at level 0 (a value
at Nat's predecessor).  `Fin (S n)` applies Fin to (S n) which
is at level 0 — different from Fin's level 1.  `LevelTear`.

The fix isn't parser-side.  Arrow-kinded data (`Fin : Nat ->
*0`) lets the level layer know Fin's argument should be at
Nat's level (level 1, post-predLv).  Or level-unification with
parametric levels could bridge.  Either is its own arc.

The parser change here is the SHAPE-layer fix; the LEVEL-layer
fix is the next move when real GADTs land.
